135
00:09:44,150 --> 00:09:49,510
And one thing I didn't mention when I introduced W3C is there are two types of standards in. the end.

136
00:09:49,510 --> 00:09:51,990
Two main categories.

137
00:09:51,990 --> 00:09:55,110
There are the jury standards and de facto standards to families.

138
00:09:55,110 --> 00:09:59,110
The jury standards are the ones that are imposed by some kind of regulation.

139
00:09:59,110 --> 00:10:03,829
So it's usually a standard you see imposed by governments for example.

140
00:10:03,829 --> 00:10:05,990
So when you look at your electric plug at home,

141
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:10,150
there's some regulation there that says this plug needs to have these properties.

142
00:10:10,160 --> 00:10:12,870
and that's a standard that gets imposed.

143
00:10:12,880 --> 00:10:15,110
It's a danger jury standard.

144
00:10:15,110 --> 00:10:17,030
And then there are de facto standards.

145
00:10:17,030 --> 00:10:25,190
which are really uh specifications that are out there and people agree to use it to implement it.

146
00:10:25,190 --> 00:10:30,870
and by virtue of being used and implemented and used by everyone they become standards.

147
00:10:30,870 --> 00:10:33,750
The WC really is in the second part.

148
00:10:33,750 --> 00:10:36,471
It's a de facto standard. IETF is the same thing.

149
00:10:36,471 --> 00:10:40,710
Some of our standards are used in are referenced in regulations now.

150
00:10:40,710 --> 00:10:44,949
but just a minority of them most of them are de facto standards.

151
00:10:44,949 --> 00:10:49,269
and that's important because that's in the end it doesn't matter what the specification says even.

152
00:10:49,279 --> 00:10:51,910
though it's a bit confusing.

153
00:10:51,910 --> 00:10:59,430
what matters is that the what the specification says matches what implementations actually implement.

154
00:10:59,430 --> 00:11:04,389
and that these implementations are used and are used interoperably across browsers for example.

155
00:11:04,389 --> 00:11:06,470
or across implementations across users.

156
00:11:06,470 --> 00:11:08,069
cross usages.

157
00:11:08,079 --> 00:11:11,269
So standardization is a lengthy process.

